SOURCE
id: 1
ts: 2026-05-29T00:00:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Fine-grained and Accurate Source Code Differencing (GumTree)
authors: Jean-Rémy Falleri, Floréal Morandat, Xavier Blanc, Matias Martinez, Martin Monperrus
year: 2014
venue: ASE '14 — ACM/IEEE International Conference on Automated Software Engineering, Västeras, Sweden, pp. 313–324
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://hal.science/hal-01054552/document
keywords: AST differencing, GumTree, tree edit distance, edit script, node matching, top-down, bottom-up, insert/delete/move/update
cites: Zhang-Shasha 1989
cited_by: TOSEM 2024 refactoring-aware diff paper, numerous subsequent AST diff tools
content:
  ALGORITHM — Three phases:
  Phase 1 (top-down greedy): Searches for isomorphic subtrees between T1 and T2.
    Uses a priority queue ordered by subtree height.
    For each candidate pair of subtrees, checks if they are isomorphic (same type, same label, same structure).
    Adds matched subtree pairs to mapping set M.
  Phase 2 (bottom-up): Propagates mappings from matched subtrees upward to their parents.
    A parent node is matched if a "significant number" of its descendants are already matched.
    Significance threshold is configurable (default: 0.5 of max descendants).
  Phase 3 (recovery/last chance): For each new parent mapping from phase 2, searches for
    recovery mappings among still-unmatched descendants using an optimal matching (DP).
  EDIT OPERATIONS: 4 types: Insert, Delete, Move, Update.
    Insert: node in T2 not matched in T1.
    Delete: node in T1 not matched in T2.
    Move: matched nodes whose parents differ.
    Update: matched nodes whose labels differ.
  EDIT SCRIPT GENERATION: Once mapping M is computed, edit script is derived by:
    1. Deleting unmatched nodes in T1 (post-order traversal).
    2. Inserting/moving nodes not in T1 or with different parents.
    3. Updating nodes with changed labels.
  COMPLEXITY: Matching is O(n^2) worst case; greedy top-down phase reduces this in practice.
  IMPLEMENTATION: GumTreeDiff GitHub — https://github.com/GumTreeDiff/gumtree
    Supports multiple parsers: JDT (Java), srcML, ANTLR, tree-sitter.
    Output: edit script in JSON/XML; also integrates with IDE plugins.
  LATER EXTENSION (ICSE 2024): "Fine-grained, accurate and scalable source differencing"
    URL: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3597503.3639148
    Falleri et al., adds scalability improvements.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 2
ts: 2026-05-29T00:00:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Simple Fast Algorithms for the Editing Distance between Trees and Related Problems (Zhang-Shasha)
authors: Kaizhong Zhang, Dennis Shasha
year: 1989
venue: SIAM Journal on Computing, Vol. 18, No. 6, pp. 1245–1262, December 1989
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://grantjenks.com/wiki/_media/ideas/simple_fast_algorithms_for_the_editing_distance_between_tree_and_related_problems.pdf
keywords: tree edit distance, ordered labeled trees, dynamic programming, insert, delete, relabel, cost function
cites: []
cited_by: GumTree 2014, RTED 2012, Autochrome, difftastic, nearly all AST diff literature
content:
  PROBLEM: Given two ordered labeled trees T1, T2, find the minimum cost sequence of
    edit operations (insert, delete, relabel) to transform T1 into T2.
  OPERATIONS:
    Delete: Remove a node v; make v's children the children of v's parent.
    Insert: Inverse of delete.
    Relabel: Change the label of a node.
  COST: Each operation has a non-negative cost. Total edit distance = sum of costs.
  ALGORITHM: Dynamic programming over "keyroots" and LRKeyroots.
    keyroot(T) = set of nodes k such that there is no node k' > k with lmost(k') = lmost(k),
    where lmost(v) = leftmost leaf descendant of v.
    Main recurrence: treedist(i, j) built from forestdist(i, j).
    forestdist(i, j) = min of:
      forestdist(lmost(i)-1, j) + deletion_cost  [delete node i]
      forestdist(i, lmost(j)-1) + insertion_cost  [insert node j]
      forestdist(lmost(i)-1, lmost(j)-1) + treedist(i, j)  [match forests]
  COMPLEXITY: O(|T1|·|T2|·min(depth(T1),leaves(T1))·min(depth(T2),leaves(T2))) time,
    O(|T1|·|T2|) space.
  CONSTRAINT: Ordered trees only (sibling order matters). For unordered trees the problem
    is NP-complete (Shasha & Zhang 1994, JACM).
  JGRAPHT IMPLEMENTATION: ZhangShashaTreeEditDistance class in org.jgrapht.alg.similarity.
  NOTE: RTED (Pawlik & Augsten 2012, arxiv:1201.0230) improves to O(|T1|·|T2|·min(path(T1),path(T2)))
    worst case. APTED (Pawlik & Augsten 2015) further improves average case.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 3
ts: 2026-05-29T00:00:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Identifying the Semantic and Textual Differences between Two Versions of a Program
authors: Susan Horwitz
year: 1990
venue: Proceedings of ACM SIGPLAN '90 Conference on Programming Language Design and Implementation (PLDI), June 1990
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~horwitz/
keywords: semantic differencing, program dependence graph, PDG, slicing, program versions, semantic equivalence, textual differences
cites: Ferrante-Ottenstein-Warren 1987 (PDG), Weiser 1984 (slicing)
cited_by: JDiff 2004, change-impact analysis surveys, Bates-Horwitz incremental testing 1993
content:
  CORE IDEA: Two programs P1 and P2 are semantically different if for some input, they
    produce different output. Textual differences (different source text) may or may not
    imply semantic differences.
  REPRESENTATION: Uses Program Dependence Graphs (PDGs).
    PDG nodes = statements + predicate expressions.
    PDG edges = data dependence (def-use) + control dependence.
  ALGORITHM:
    1. Build PDGs for P1 and P2.
    2. Identify textually changed statements by comparing ASTs.
    3. For each changed statement s: compute forward slice of P1 from s and forward slice
       of P2 from s. If slices differ, output is semantically different.
    4. Unchanged statements surrounded by identical context → no semantic difference.
  KEY INSIGHT: A statement s in P1 corresponds to statement s' in P2 (textually equivalent)
    even if its dependence edges changed — the dependence edges encode which definitions
    reach s, i.e., which values flow to it.
  APPLICATION TO DIFF: "Which statements in P1 might produce different output in P2?"
    = set of statements reachable via changed def-use / control-dep edges from textually
    changed statements.
  NOTE: This is the foundational paper for semantic (as opposed to textual) program diff.
    Subsequent work: Bates & Horwitz 1993 (incremental testing using PDGs, POPL).
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 4
ts: 2026-05-29T00:00:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Interprocedural Slicing Using Dependence Graphs
authors: Susan Horwitz, Thomas Reps, David Binkley
year: 1990
venue: ACM Transactions on Programming Languages and Systems, 12(1):26–60, January 1990
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.csa.iisc.ac.in/~raghavan/CleanedPav2011/horwitz-sdg-slicing-1990.pdf
keywords: system dependence graph, SDG, PDG, interprocedural slicing, def-use, call graph, summary edges
cites: Ferrante-Ottenstein-Warren 1987, Weiser 1984
cited_by: Reps-Horwitz-Sagiv 1995 (IFDS), JDiff, nearly all interprocedural analysis literature
content:
  SDG = System Dependence Graph: collection of PDGs (one per procedure) + inter-procedure edges.
  PDG for procedure p contains:
    - Entry vertex
    - Statement vertices (one per statement/predicate)
    - Data dependence edges (def-use within procedure)
    - Control dependence edges
    - Summary edges: encode transitive effects of called procedures (def→use across call site)
  SLICING ALGORITHM on SDG:
    Phase 1: Traverse backward from slice criterion, following data+control+summary edges,
      but NOT ascending call edges (avoids call-return confusion).
    Phase 2: Traverse backward following all edge types including call edges.
    Two-phase avoids counting paths that go up a call edge then down a different return edge.
  RELEVANCE TO DIFF: The summary edges encode "which output variables of callee are affected
    by which input parameters" — when a callee changes, summary edges must be recomputed.
    This is exactly the interprocedural change propagation problem in pflow diff.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 5
ts: 2026-05-29T00:00:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: JDiff: A Differencing Technique and Tool for Object-Oriented Programs
authors: Taweesap Apiwattanapong, Alessandro Orso, Mary Jean Harrold
year: 2004/2006
venue: ASE 2004; Automated Software Engineering journal 2006. DOI: 10.1007/s10515-006-0002-0
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/JDiff:-A-differencing-technique-and-tool-for-Apiwattanapong-Orso/caf7737b93748839ec1a8e8bfaccc14ba2385e4e
keywords: JDiff, CFG differencing, ECFG, hammock, Java, OO programs, change impact, regression testing
cites: Horwitz 1990, CFG literature
cited_by: Change-impact analysis surveys, regression testing papers
content:
  REPRESENTATION: Extended CFG (ECFG) handles OO features: exceptions, polymorphism,
    field accesses, virtual dispatch.
    ECFG nodes = statements + predicates.
    ECFG edges = control flow + exception edges + virtual dispatch edges.
  HAMMOCK DECOMPOSITION:
    A hammock H(s, e) = subgraph with single entry node s and single exit node e,
    where all paths from entry to exit are contained in H.
    Algorithm: Identify SCCs, then decompose into hammocks recursively.
    Hammocks correspond to structured control flow: if/else, loops, try/catch blocks.
  MATCHING ALGORITHM (between P1 and P2):
    1. Match methods by signature (name + parameter types).
    2. For each matched method pair, build ECFGs.
    3. Reduce each ECFG to hammock tree.
    4. Recursively compare corresponding hammocks top-down.
    5. Two hammocks are "equivalent" if their node sequences and edge structures match.
    6. If a hammock differs, classify as: INS, DEL, MOD (modified).
  OUTPUT: Set of changed methods + changed hammocks within methods + changed statements.
  EXCEPTION EDGES: Explicit modeling of throw→catch edges in ECFG.
    A changed exception edge = "new exception path from X to finally handler".
  CHANGE IMPACT: After differencing, propagate changes through call graph to find
    all transitively affected methods.
  TOOL: JDiff Java tool, available for download; used in regression test selection.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 6
ts: 2026-05-29T00:00:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: difftastic — Structural diff that understands syntax
authors: Wilfred Hughes
year: 2022–present
venue: Open source tool, GitHub
quality: CODE
url: https://github.com/Wilfred/difftastic
keywords: difftastic, tree-sitter, structural diff, Dijkstra, graph traversal, syntax-aware, 60+ languages
cites: Autochrome (algorithm inspiration), tree-sitter
cited_by: widely used in git workflows
content:
  PARSING PIPELINE:
    Stage 1: tree-sitter parser → concrete syntax tree (CST).
    Stage 2: CST → difftastic Syntax tree (simplified representation retaining
      structural info needed for diffing; discards some CST detail).
  DIFF ALGORITHM:
    Treats tree comparison as graph traversal problem.
    Graph nodes = (position in T1, position in T2).
    Graph edges = possible operations: advance T1 (delete), advance T2 (insert), advance both (match).
    Edge weights = cost of operation (0 for match, 1 for insert/delete).
    Finds minimum cost path using Dijkstra's algorithm.
    Result: sequence of matched/unmatched nodes across both trees.
  NODE MATCHING: Two nodes match if they have the same type AND their contents are
    "similar enough" (configurable threshold).
    Delimiter nodes (brackets, braces) are matched pair-wise.
  COMPARISON TO GUMTREE:
    GumTree: greedy top-down + bottom-up, produces move operations.
    difftastic: graph search on leaves, no explicit move operation (shows as delete+insert).
    diffsitter: LCS on leaves only.
    difftastic treats diff as graph search, avoids GumTree's quadratic worst case on large files.
  LANGUAGE SUPPORT: 60+ languages via tree-sitter grammars.
  SOURCE: src/parse/tree_sitter_parser.rs (parser), src/diff/ (algorithm)
  MANUAL: https://difftastic.wilfred.me.uk/tree_diffing.html
  NOTE: No "move" operation — only insert/delete/match. Moves appear as delete+insert pair.
<<<END>>>

