relevance: HIGH
title: SemanticDiff — Language-aware diff for VS Code and GitHub
authors: SemanticDiff team
year: 2022–present
venue: Commercial tool
quality: INDUSTRY
url: https://semanticdiff.com/
keywords: SemanticDiff, AST diff, VS Code extension, refactoring detection, whitespace-invariant, Python, Rust, Java, C#, TypeScript
cites: []
cited_by: []
content:
  PIPELINE (3 stages):
    1. Parse old and new code → AST.
    2. Compare ASTs: verify that structurally different-looking statements are semantically
       identical (using AST equality not text equality).
    3. Generate visual diff output: align old/new source using node mapping.
       Unmatched old nodes → marked deleted. Unmatched new nodes → marked inserted.
  FEATURES:
    - Hides irrelevant changes: whitespace, optional commas, unnecessary parentheses.
    - Detects moved code, renames, refactorings.
    - Supports: Python, Rust, Java, C#, TypeScript, and more.
  DIFFERENTIATOR: "Programming language aware diff that distinguishes between relevant
    and irrelevant changes."
  INTEGRATION: VS Code extension + GitHub integration.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 9
ts: 2026-05-29T00:00:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: A Novel Refactoring and Semantic Aware AST Differencing Tool
authors: Nikolaos Tsantalis et al.
year: 2024
venue: ACM Transactions on Software Engineering and Methodology. DOI: 10.1145/3696002
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://arxiv.org/html/2403.05939
keywords: AST differencing, refactoring awareness, RefactoringMiner, multi-mapping, semantic compatibility, commit-level diff
cites: GumTree 2014, RefactoringMiner
cited_by: []
content:
  FIVE LIMITATIONS ADDRESSED:
    1. Lacking multi-mapping support (1:N node mappings).
    2. Matching semantically incompatible AST nodes (e.g., matching a method call
       to a variable reference when they serve different roles).
    3. Ignoring language clues (e.g., method signatures, type information).
    4. Lacking refactoring awareness (renames/moves confuse existing matchers).
    5. Lacking commit-level diff support (single file vs. multi-file commits).
  APPROACH:
    Based on RefactoringMiner: detects refactoring instances first (rename, move, extract method, etc.).
    Improved RefactoringMiner for higher statement mapping accuracy.
    Then generates AST diff for a given commit/PR based on refactoring instances
    and pairs of matched program element declarations.
  BENCHMARK: First benchmark of AST node mappings:
    800 bug-fixing commits + 188 refactoring commits.
  RESULTS: "Considerably higher precision and recall, especially for refactoring commits,
    with execution time comparable to faster tools."
  RELEVANCE: For pflow diff — handle renames/moves of functions/variables as semantic
    equivalences, not as delete+insert (which would falsely report all flow changed).
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 10
ts: 2026-05-29T00:00:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Incremental Data Flow Analysis (Ryder & Paull 1988)
authors: Barbara G. Ryder, Marvin C. Paull
year: 1988
venue: ACM Transactions on Programming Languages and Systems, 10(1):1–50
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/42192.42193
keywords: incremental dataflow, IDFA, live variables, reaching definitions, ud-chains, worklist, change propagation
cites: Kildall 1973, Aho-Ullman dataflow
cited_by: Common Threads survey 2024, subsequent incremental analysis papers
content:
  CORE PROBLEM: After a small change to program P to get P', recompute dataflow facts
    using previously computed results. Avoid full reanalysis.
  ALGORITHM OVERVIEW:
    1. Identify changed nodes in CFG (insertions, deletions, modifications of basic blocks).
    2. Initialize change set ΔX = set of nodes whose dataflow facts are invalidated.
    3. Propagate changes through CFG using worklist algorithm:
       For each node in worklist: recompute dataflow function. If result changed, add
       successors (for forward analysis) or predecessors (for backward analysis) to worklist.
    4. Terminate when worklist empty (fixed point reached for changed region).
  KEY INSIGHT: Only nodes reachable from changed nodes (in dataflow propagation order)
    need recomputation. Unchanged nodes with unchanged inputs → skip.
  ANALYSES COVERED: Reaching definitions, live variables, available expressions, du-chains.
  COMPLEXITY: O(k * |ΔG|) where k = depth of dataflow lattice, |ΔG| = size of changed subgraph.
    Much better than O(k * |G|) for small changes.
  RELATION TO pflow diff: The same incremental-recomputation structure applies to
    recomputing which def-use edges changed after an edit.
<<<END>>>

