SOURCE
id: 15
ts: 2026-05-29T00:01:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: GumTree Algorithm — Detailed Technical Notes (Phase-by-Phase)
authors: Jean-Rémy Falleri et al. (documented by hoblovski blog post)
year: 2018 (blog post of 2014 paper)
venue: BLOG post summarizing ASE 2014 paper
quality: BLOG
url: https://hoblovski.github.io/2018/05/31/GumTree.html
keywords: GumTree, dice coefficient, anchor mapping, container mapping, recovery mapping, isomorphism hash, opt algorithm
cites: GumTree ASE 2014
cited_by: []
content:
  AST STRUCTURE: Ordered n-ary trees. Each node has label (type) + value (string).
  
  SIMILARITY METRIC — Dice Coefficient:
    dice(t1, t2, M) = 2|M| / (|t1| + |t2|)
    where M = current mapping set, |t| = number of descendant leaves in subtree t.
    Range: [0, 1]. Used in phases 2 and 3 to select best match candidate.
  
  PHASE 1 — Anchor Mapping (Top-Down Greedy):
    Only considers subtrees with height >= minHeight (default: > 1, i.e., not leaf nodes).
    Uses hash fingerprinting for isomorphism detection:
      H(s) = f(K, H(t1), H(t2), ..., H(tn))
      where K = root node hash, H(ti) = child hashes, f = MD5 or SHA1-based combiner.
      Isomorphic subtrees have identical hashes → O(1) comparison.
    For unique (t1, t2) isomorphic pairs: add directly to M.
    For ambiguous cases (multiple candidates): select pair with highest
      dice(parent(t1), parent(t2), M) — uses parent context to break ties.
    Result: M contains mappings for large unchanged subtrees.
  
  PHASE 2 — Container Mapping (Bottom-Up):
    For each node t1 in T1 (post-order): find best matching t2 in T2 such that:
      - t1, t2 have same type/label.
      - t1 does not already have anchor mapping.
      - t1 has at least one descendant with anchor mapping.
    Selection: t2 = argmax dice(t1, t2, M).
    Threshold: only add to M if dice > minDice (default: 0.5).
    Result: M extended with "container" node matches.
  
  PHASE 3 — Recovery Mapping (Bottom-Up using opt):
    For each pair (t1, t2) in M from phase 2: run opt algorithm on their
    unmatched descendants.
    opt = O(n^3) optimal tree edit distance WITHOUT move operations.
    Adds fine-grained mappings for remaining unmatched nodes.
  
  EDIT SCRIPT GENERATION:
    From M, produces edit script:
      INS(t2, p2, k): Insert node t2 as k-th child of p2.
      DEL(t1): Delete node t1.
      MOV(t1, p2, k): Move t1 to be k-th child of p2.
      UPD(t1, v2): Update label/value of t1 to v2.
    Algorithm: BFS on T2, for each node check if matched/unmatched/moved/updated.
  
  DESIGN PHILOSOPHY: "Rather than finding algorithmically optimal edit scripts,
    the approach aims to reflect developer intent through engineering heuristics."
    — Exact quote from source blog post.
<<<END>>>

