id: 17
ts: 2026-05-29T00:01:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Scaling Static Analyses at Facebook (Infer compositional/incremental model)
authors: Dino Distefano, Manuel Fahndrich, Francesco Logozzo, Peter O'Hearn
year: 2019
venue: Communications of the ACM, 62(8):62–70, August 2019
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://cacm.acm.org/research/scaling-static-analyses-at-facebook/
keywords: Infer, compositional analysis, bi-abduction, incremental, procedure summaries, Facebook, code review, separation logic
cites: O'Hearn 2012 (bi-abduction), Reynolds 2002 (separation logic)
cited_by: Infer documentation, incremental analysis papers
content:
  KEY INSIGHT: "Compositional analyses are naturally incremental — changing one procedure
    does not necessitate re-analyzing all other procedures."
  
  BI-ABDUCTION (Infer's core analysis):
    For each procedure p: compute summary (precondition, postcondition) independently of callers.
    Summary = separation logic spec: {pre} p {post}.
    At call sites: apply summary via frame rule.
    When p changes: recompute only p's summary + summaries of direct callers (if call signature changes).
  
  INCREMENTAL MODEL at Facebook:
    Infer integrated with code review system.
    "Even a linear time analysis would be too slow if it had to operate on the whole program
    when presented with a code change."
    Only changed procedures + their transitive callers are re-analyzed.
    Procedure summaries are cached and reused for unchanged procedures.
  
  PROCEDURE MATCHING ACROSS VERSIONS:
    Procedures matched by (fully-qualified name, parameter types).
    Body changes → invalidate summary → recompute.
    No body change → reuse cached summary.
  
  DIFF-LEVEL FOCUS: Infer reports only bugs in changed code in differential mode.
    (See record id:12 for reportdiff details.)
  
  RELEVANCE: The summary-caching + invalidation model is directly applicable to pflow diff:
    cache CFG/PDG for each procedure; on change, recompute only changed procedure + callers.
<<<END>>>

